--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3b. REFERENCE CONTROL -- GET https://api.openai.com/v1/models + Authorization
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Included to prove the block is not "any authenticated call" and not "any 401".
GET with an Authorization header is also preflighted (Authorization is not a
CORS-safelisted request header), and here BOTH legs carry ACAO.

  OPTIONS -> HTTP 200
    Access-Control-Allow-Origin:    *
    Access-Control-Allow-Headers:   authorization
    Access-Control-Allow-Methods:   DELETE, GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PATCH, POST, PUT
    Access-Control-Max-Age:         600
    Access-Control-Expose-Headers:  X-Request-ID, CF-Ray
  GET     -> HTTP 401
    Access-Control-Allow-Origin:    *
    Access-Control-Expose-Headers:  X-Request-ID, CF-Ray

  READABLE in all 4 browser x origin combos. status 401.
  Readable header names from JS: cf-ray, content-length, content-type, x-request-id
  Body shape (keys only): { error: { message: string, type: string,
                                     param: null, code: string } }
  error.code = invalid_api_key, error.type = invalid_request_error

  So a 401 produced by an invalid key is perfectly readable on /v1/models.
  The three inference POST endpoints behave differently.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. WSS wss://api.openai.com/v1/realtime?model=gpt-realtime-mini
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WebSocket handshakes are not subject to CORS, so this leg is expected to behave
differently from fetch -- and it does.

4a. Documented BETA browser subprotocol form
    subprotocols: ["realtime", "openai-insecure-api-key.sk-****",
                   "openai-beta.realtime-v1"]
  All 4 combos (chromium/webkit x hosted/file), identical:
    handshake ACCEPTED (onopen fired), negotiated subprotocol = "realtime"
    first server frame:
      {"type":"error","event_id":"event_...","error":{
        "type":"invalid_request_error",
        "code":"beta_api_shape_disabled",
        "message":"The Realtime Beta API is no longer supported. Please use
                   /v1/realtime for the GA API.",
        "param":null,"event_id":null}}
    close code 4000, reason "invalid_request_error.beta_api_shape_disabled",
    wasClean true
  This is an API-SHAPE refusal, not CORS and not auth. The beta subprotocol
  form documented for browsers is dead.

4b. GA form (same URL, openai-beta subprotocol dropped)
    subprotocols: ["realtime", "openai-insecure-api-key.sk-****"]
  All 4 combos, identical:
    handshake ACCEPTED (onopen fired), negotiated subprotocol = "realtime"
    first server frame:
      {"type":"error","event_id":"event_...","error":{
        "type":"invalid_request_error",
        "code":"invalid_api_key",
        "message":"Incorrect API key provided: sk-**** ... ",
           (server echoes its own mask of the invalid key; re-masked here)
        "param":null,"event_id":null}}
    close code 3000, reason "invalid_request_error.invalid_api_key",
    wasClean true

  HANDSHAKE VERDICT: NOT a CORS-shaped refusal. The socket opens from a browser
  page on both origins, the server speaks the Realtime event protocol, and the
  only thing it objects to is the deliberately invalid key. With the GA
  subprotocol form the failure is a clean AUTH refusal (code invalid_api_key,
  close 3000). That is the strongest possible phase-1 signal short of a real
  key: browser-direct Realtime over WSS is reachable and auth-gated, not
  origin-gated.

  ENDPOINT VERDICT: NOT refused. Use the GA subprotocol form
  ["realtime", "openai-insecure-api-key.<key>"] -- drop "openai-beta.realtime-v1".

================================================================================
CURL CROSS-CHECK (no browser, raw headers)
================================================================================
Preflight, run for each of the three REST endpoints:

  curl -s -i -X OPTIONS https://api.openai.com/v1/<endpoint> \
    -H "Origin: https://sotto-steel.vercel.app" \
    -H "Access-Control-Request-Method: POST" \
    -H "Access-Control-Request-Headers: authorization,content-type"

  /v1/audio/transcriptions -> HTTP/2 200
      access-control-allow-headers: authorization,content-type
      access-control-allow-methods: GET, OPTIONS, POST
      access-control-allow-origin:  https://sotto-steel.vercel.app
      access-control-max-age:       86400
      access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
  /v1/chat/completions     -> HTTP/2 200   (identical values;
                                            expose-headers sent twice: CF-Ray)
  /v1/audio/speech         -> HTTP/2 200   (identical values)

  Same three with -H "Origin: null" -> HTTP/2 200,
      access-control-allow-origin: null   (server reflects it), rest identical.

  No access-control-allow-credentials on any preflight. Expose-headers is only
  CF-Ray (plus X-Request-ID on /v1/models), so x-ratelimit-* and x-request-id
  are NOT readable from page JS on the three inference endpoints even when a
  response is readable.

Actual POST, the decisive cross-check:

  WITH Authorization + Origin:
    curl -s -i -X POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions \
      -H "Origin: https://sotto-steel.vercel.app" \
      -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-invalid-spike-key" \
      -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{}'
    -> HTTP/2 401
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
       *** NO access-control-allow-origin ***
    Identical result for /v1/audio/speech and /v1/audio/transcriptions.

  WITHOUT Authorization, same Origin:
    -> HTTP/2 401
       access-control-allow-origin: *
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
    Identical for all three endpoints.

  Reference: GET /v1/models WITH Authorization + Origin
    -> HTTP/2 401
       access-control-allow-origin: *
       access-control-expose-headers: X-Request-ID, CF-Ray

The curl trace reproduces the browser result exactly and off the browser stack:
Access-Control-Allow-Origin is present on the preflight and on unauthenticated
POST responses, and absent on authenticated POST responses to the three
inference endpoints.

================================================================================
VERDICT
================================================================================
Browser-direct BYOK is REFUSED for all three REST endpoints --
POST /v1/audio/transcriptions, POST /v1/chat/completions and
POST /v1/audio/speech -- in both iPhone-shaped WebKit 26.5 and desktop
Chromium 151, from both the hosted https://sotto-steel.vercel.app origin and a
file:// (Origin: null) page. The refusal is not a preflight refusal: every
OPTIONS returns 200 with a correct Access-Control-Allow-Origin (the exact
origin, or the literal "null" for file://), Access-Control-Allow-Headers
covering authorization and content-type, Allow-Methods "GET, OPTIONS, POST" and
Max-Age 86400. The refusal happens on the real response: as soon as an
Authorization header is on the request, api.openai.com returns the response
with no Access-Control-Allow-Origin at all, and the browser drops it before page
JS sees it -- Chromium reports corsError MissingAllowOriginHeader, WebKit
reports the opaque "Load failed", and page JS can read neither the status nor
the JSON body. The identical request with the Authorization header removed
returns Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * and is fully readable, and preflighted
GET /v1/models with the same invalid key is fully readable, so this is neither a
generic 401 path nor a network artifact -- it is specific to authenticated calls
to the inference POST endpoints, which is exactly the BYOK shape. The Realtime
WebSocket is the one exception and it is NOT refused: with the GA subprotocol
form ["realtime", "openai-insecure-api-key.<key>"] the handshake is accepted
from a browser page on both origins in both browsers, the server negotiates the
"realtime" subprotocol and speaks its event protocol, and the only objection is
the deliberately invalid key (error.code invalid_api_key, close 3000) -- a clean
auth refusal, not a CORS-shaped one. Note separately that the documented BETA
browser form (adding "openai-beta.realtime-v1") is now dead: the server accepts
the socket and immediately errors beta_api_shape_disabled with close 4000, so
any Realtime client must drop that subprotocol.

CONSEQUENCE FOR ROUTE B: escalation condition met for the REST leg. Route B
cannot be "the PWA calls api.openai.com directly with the user's key" for
transcription, chat or speech; those must go through a relay (route B via C's
server, or any origin the user controls). The Realtime WSS leg does NOT need a
relay and can stay browser-direct.

HONEST LIMIT ON THIS CONCLUSION: every REST observation above is on the
invalid-key 401 path, because no key exists yet. It is possible, though not
supported by any evidence here, that a 200 response on the same endpoints carries
Access-Control-Allow-Origin when the 401 does not. Phase 2 settles it in one
run. The evidence against that possibility is that GET /v1/models returns ACAO
on its 401, so a 401 as such does not strip the header.

